{
  "abstract": "We prove that every biplanar graph with two universal vertices on at most twenty-four vertices admits a proper nine-coloring. The two universal vertices leave seven colors for the remaining body. A smallest counterexample at order twenty-four would have a body with twenty-two vertices, independence number four and 87 edges. We extend colorings across the exterior of a smallest seven-chromatic induced subgraph after an independent deletion. A prescribed singleton color on an Ore composition resolves the final small subgraphs. The remaining deletions force incompatible degrees on overlapping or disjoint copies of a four-clique joined to a pentagon. We then consider a universal vertex and a second vertex whose only nonneighbors are two adjacent exceptions. A nine-coloring exists exactly when the body nonneighbors of one exception contain an independent set meeting every minimal seven-chromatic subgraph of the body. For every pair of these subgraphs such a set exists at one exception, with no order restriction. Through order twenty-five, failure for a prescribed pair at one exception forces at most six body neighbors at the other. We also prove nine-colorability at this order bound whenever at most four body nonneighbors of either exception are nonisolated among themselves. The supplement includes the proofs, exact enumeration programs, certificates for the spanning-deletion cases and a fourteen-vertex drawing with both endpoint choices.",
